# Hexagonal Architecture

Supports `.project-overview.mdc` and `architecture.mdc`. Every TypeScript **business** module and the Python AI service uses hexagonal dependency rules.

TypeScript business modules:

```
<module>/
  domain/
  application/
  adapters/
    inbound/
    outbound/
```

The TypeScript `modules/ai` package is an **integration boundary**, not an AI domain. It only needs `application/ports` (`AIServicePort`) and `adapters/outbound/python-ai` (`PythonAIServiceAdapter`).

Python AI (`apps/ai/app/`): `domain/`, `application/`, and `adapters/` are the hexagonal layers. Capability packages (`agents/`, `orchestration/`, `rag/`, `llm/`, `embeddings/`, `vector_store/`, `tools/`, `prompts/`, `guardrails/`, `evaluation/`) are application/domain logic and must not import provider SDKs.

## Dependency rule

**Adapters → Application → Domain**

| Layer | May import | Must not import |
|---|---|---|
| Domain | domain only | Fastify, FastAPI, Prisma, SQLAlchemy, Redis, queues, LLM SDKs, LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex, HTTP |
| Application / AI capability packages | domain + port interfaces | Prisma, FastAPI, Fastify, provider SDKs |
| Adapters | application ports + domain types to map | other modules’ adapters or Prisma models |

## Ports

Infrastructure implements interfaces defined by inner layers. If a use case needs I/O, add a port first, then an adapter.

## Inbound vs outbound

- Inbound: REST, WebSocket, webhooks, queue/event consumers — call use cases
- Outbound (TypeScript): Postgres/Prisma, Redis, email, WhatsApp, queues, `PythonAIServiceAdapter`
- Outbound (Python AI): LLM, embeddings, vector DB under `adapters/llm/`, `adapters/embeddings/`, `adapters/vector_store/` — never Prisma

Controllers stay thin. Never Controller → Prisma, Controller → OpenAI, or Controller → business rules. LLM / embedding / vector SDKs belong only in Python AI outbound adapters. Do not install every provider now.

## Modules

Do not flatten the app into global `services/` or `repositories/`. Keep logic inside the owning module. Cross-module access is contracts, ports, or events only. TypeScript ↔ Python uses `packages/contracts`, not shared internals.
